\section{Questions a reader may ask}\label{app:faq}
\textbf{Is [-AP-Q-]{+GPT A22+} simply a worse model?} Per training token, yes (Table~\ref{tab:results}). Per GPU-second its difference from AR-ref is not resolved (300\,s, E1) while it is \CRParamReduction\% smaller, {+trains \CRNineTrainRatio$\times$ as fast and, on the original 256-token benchmark,+} decodes \CRDecodeRatio$\times$ faster [-and uses-]{+with+} \CRMemoryReduction\% less inference memory. Which matters depends on whether compute time or data is the scarce resource.

\textbf{Why not use a fixed-token contract from the start?} A fixed-time contract is the autoresearch standard and answers a practical question: the best model from a fixed amount of GPU time. It is not wrong, but it scores a program's throughput and its per-token quality together. A fixed-token contract isolates per-token quality but lets compute and update count vary. We report both.

\textbf{Why did every agent shrink the model?} Under 300\,s, BPB improves with more updates and tokens, and a smaller model gets more of both. The agents' hypotheses say so (Table~\ref{tab:reasoning}). E1 prices the trade for [-AP-Q:-]{+GPT A22:+} it trains \CREOneRho$\times$ faster but needs about \CREOneK$\times$ AR-ref's tokens for equal quality, so at 300\,s the two roughly cancel. Opus's recipes would need more than \CRMTKBound$\times$ AR-ref's tokens, about their speed advantage, and post hoc, at 201.3M tokens they do not differ resolvably from AR-ref at 300\,s (95\% CIs span zero).

\textbf{Does E1 prove that exposure is the only cause?} No. E1 shows that at equal tokens and updates [-AP-Q-]{+GPT A22+} is worse, on the same seeds and data, and its exploratory split attributes the 300\,s result to extra tokens offsetting a weaker architecture. Tokens and updates move together, and architecture and optimizer edits are bundled, so finer causes are not separated.

\textbf{Are three seeds enough?} For the matched-token gap, yes: \CREOneMatchedLoAbs--\CREOneMatchedHiAbs{} held-out BPB against a within-recipe seed SD of at most \CREOneMaxSD, on all \CREOnePairs{} seed--budget pairs, and the panel result replicates on other seeds and another A40. For the slope of the gap with tokens, or to show equivalence at 300\,s, no.

